# Files every pipeline writes into the working tree, whatever the language.
#
# `global/scripts/shared/cleanup.sh` creates REPORT_PATH before each tool runs and
# defaults it to `build/reports`; a language fragment that overrides REPORT_PATH
# ships its own entry for the directory it chose instead.
build/reports/

# CodeQL builds its database inside the project it is analysing
# (`global/scripts/tools/codeql/run.sh`) and removes it only on the happy path, so
# an interrupted or failing run leaves it behind.
.codeql-db/

# `global/scripts/tools/semgrep/run.sh` copies its default `.semgrepignore` into the
# project root when the project ships none, and removes it again on the way out -- but
# only on the way out. Four `exit 1` paths between the copy and the removal (no Python 3,
# the virtualenv cannot be created, `pip` cannot install the pinned Semgrep, the installed
# version is not the pinned one) leave it behind with no signal at all, and so does any
# interruption of the scan itself, which is the longest-running step in the suite. It then
# sits in the working tree as an untracked file that `git add -A` will stage -- it has
# already come close to being committed that way in a consumer.
#
# A project that wants its OWN `.semgrepignore` is unaffected: the runner copies the
# default only when the file is absent, and once the project's copy is committed it is a
# tracked file, which `.gitignore` does not apply to. To start tracking a new one, either
# `git add -f .semgrepignore` -- which is what git's own error message tells you -- or add
# `!.semgrepignore` below this block, where a later match wins.
.semgrepignore
